The Roadmap Epigenomics Project provides a comprehensive reference collection
of human epigenomes for key cellular states relevant to human health and
disease. The project generated genome-wide maps of DNA methylation, histone
modifications, chromatin accessibility, and RNA expression across 111
reference epigenomes.
}
\usage{
roadmap_bed_gz_links_metadata
}
\description{
A named list containing download URLs and metadata for Roadmap Epigenomics
BED.gz files organized by data type. This dataset includes URLs for promoter,
enhancer, and ChromHMM state annotations, along with merged metadata from
\code{\link{roadmap_metadata}} for each epigenome.
}
\details{
This dataset was generated by scraping the Roadmap Epigenomics web portal
to extract download links for BED.gz files. The data is organized into three
categories:
\itemize{
  \item \strong{DHS_promoter}: DNase I hypersensitive sites (DHS) in promoter
        regions. Files are located at:
        \url{https://egg2.wustl.edu/roadmap/data/byDataType/dnase/BED_files_prom/}
  \item \strong{DHS_enhancer}: DNase I hypersensitive sites (DHS) in enhancer
        regions. Files are located at:
        \url{https://egg2.wustl.edu/roadmap/data/byDataType/dnase/BED_files_enh/}
  \item \strong{ChromHMM_15_states_5_coreMarks}: Chromatin state segmentations
        using a 15-state ChromHMM model with 5 core marks. Files are located at:
        \url{https://egg2.wustl.edu/roadmap/data/byFileType/chromhmmSegmentations/ChmmModels/coreMarks/jointModel/final/}
}

The dataset was created using the following process:

\preformatted{
# Load roadmap_metadata for merging
data("roadmap_metadata")

# Scrape URLs for each data type
url_prom <- "https://egg2.wustl.edu/roadmap/data/byDataType/dnase/BED_files_prom/"
url_enh <- "https://egg2.wustl.edu/roadmap/data/byDataType/dnase/BED_files_enh/"
url_chromhmm <- "https://egg2.wustl.edu/roadmap/data/byFileType/chromhmmSegmentations/ChmmModels/coreMarks/jointModel/final/"

# Extract URLs and EIDs, then merge with metadata
roadmap_bed_gz_links_metadata <- lapply(
    list(url_prom, url_enh, url_chromhmm),
    function(.url) {
        urls <- scrape_roadmap_bed_files(.url)
        EID <- gsub(".+(E\\d+).*", "\\1", urls)
        urls <- data.frame(EID = EID, URL = urls, stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
        urls <- merge(urls, roadmap_metadata, 
                     by.x = "EID", by.y = "epigenome_id_eid", all.x = TRUE)
    }
)
names(roadmap_bed_gz_links_metadata) <- c("DHS_promoter", 
                                          "DHS_enhancer", 
                                          "ChromHMM_15_states_5_coreMarks")

# Save as package data
usethis::use_data(roadmap_bed_gz_links_metadata, overwrite = TRUE)
}

\strong{Important Notes:}
\itemize{
  \item All BED files are based on the \strong{hg19} genome assembly.
  \item If your peaks use a different assembly (e.g., hg38), the
        \code{\link{annotatePeaksWithRoadmap}} function will automatically
        perform coordinate conversion using \code{rtracklayer::liftOver()}.
  \item Files are compressed (.bed.gz format) and can be directly loaded
        using \code{rtracklayer::import()}.
}

This dataset is used internally by \code{\link{annotatePeaksWithRoadmap}} to:
\itemize{
  \item Retrieve download URLs for specific epigenomes and data types
  \item Filter epigenomes by tissue type (using the \code{anatomy} column)
  \item Filter epigenomes by EID
  \item Access metadata for each epigenome (names, tissue types, etc.)
}
}
